<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="approx-ints-complete">
    <title>Approximating Integrals (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Evaluating definite integrals can get hard fast! In practice, it is useful to have methods for 
                        approximating values of integrals. Typically, this takes the form 
                        <md>
                            \int_a^b f(x) \quad dx \approx \sum_{i=1}^n w_i f(x_i)
                        </md>.
                        How we determine the weights (<m>w_i</m>) and the nodes (<m>x_i</m>) used in the approximation
                        is what varies between methods. We'll look at some common methods used in quantum mechanics.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>The Trapezoid Rule</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        
                         
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        See the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/avbulpbcco">Trapezoid rule Desmos interactive </url>
                        for the visual used to determine the Trapezoid rule for approximation:
                        <md>
                            \int_a^b f(x) \quad dx  \approx \frac{\Delta x}{2}(f(x_1)+ 2 f(x_2) + \dots + 2f(x_{n-1}) + f(x_n))
                        </md>.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Example: Trapezoid Rule</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \int_{-1}^1 e^{-x^2} \quad dx</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Using <m>n=4</m> makes <m>\Delta x = 0.5</m>. Then using the trapezoid rule gives 
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\int_{-1}^1 e^{-x^2} \quad dx \amp\approx \frac{1}{4}(f(-1) + 2f(-0.5) + 2f(0) + 2f(0.5) + f(1) ) </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp\approx 1.463 </mrow>
                        </md>.
                        Compare this to the true value using technology of approximately <m>1.494</m>.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise workspace="4in">
                <title>Simpson's Rule</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        See the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/hqsw8hmrbf">Simpson's rule Desmos interactive </url>
                        for the visual used to determine Simpson's rule for approximation: For <m>n</m> even,
                        <md>
                            \int_a^b f(x) \quad dx  \approx \frac{\Delta x}{3}(f(x_0)+ \sum_{i \text{ odd}}^{n-1} 4f(x_i) + \sum_{i \text{ even}}^{n-2} 2f(x_i) + f(x_n) )
                        </md>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
              
                
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="4in">
                <title>Example: Simpson's Rule</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                     <m>\displaystyle \int_{-1}^1 e^{-x^2} \quad dx</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Using <m>n=4</m> makes <m>\Delta x = 0.5</m>. Then using the Simpson's rule gives 
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\int_{-1}^1 e^{-x^2} \quad dx \amp\approx \frac{1}{6}(f(-1) + 4f(-0.5) + 2f(0) + 4f(0.5) + f(1) ) </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp\approx 1.494 </mrow>
                        </md>
                        which is even closer to the true value than when using the Trapezoid rule.
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>

            
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise workspace="4in">
            <title>Gaussian-Hermite Quadrature</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x)e^{-x^2} \quad dx \approx \sum_{i=1}^n w_if(x_i)</m>, where
                </p>
                <image source="g-h-quad.jpeg">
                    <shortdescription>Table of nodes and weights for Gaussian-Hermite quadrature.</shortdescription>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    This method is best for improper integrals of a particular form, and actually gives <em>equality</em> when <m>f</m> is a polynomial of degree <m>2n-1</m> or less.
                </p>
                <p>
                    In lab we'll discover a bit more about where the table of weights and nodes comes from, but for now 
                    we should understand how to use them.
                </p>
            </solution>
            
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <title>Examples: Gaussian-Hermite Quadrature</title>
            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} \quad dx</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                       <md>
                        <mrow>\sum_{i=1}^2 w_i f(x_i) \amp= 0.886f(-0.707) + 0.886f(0.707) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp=2(.886) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp=1.772 (\approx \sqrt{\pi}) </mrow>
                       </md>
                       
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^\infty ae^{-\frac{(x-b)^2}{2c^2}} \quad dx</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Use the substitution <m>u = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}c}(x-b)</m>, <m>du = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}c} dx</m> to transform
                        the integral to 
                        <md>
                            \int_{-\infty}^\infty \sqrt{2}c ae^{-u^2} \quad du
                        </md>.
                        Then using the result from the previous example, we can see that 
                        <md>
                            <mrow>  \int_{-\infty}^\infty \sqrt{2}c ue^{-u^2} \quad du \amp= \sqrt{2}c a \sqrt{\pi}</mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= ac\sqrt{2\pi} </mrow>
                        </md>.
                        This result allows us to normalize a general Gaussian function!
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            
        </exercise>
    </page>

       
    </worksheet>
